  "name": "PLGF (Placental Growth Factor)",
  "code": "PLGF",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>PLGF Level (pg/mL)</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Range</div></td><td><div>A PLGF level within the normal range is typically observed in healthy pregnancies. It indicates that the placenta is functioning properly and supporting normal fetal growth and development.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Elevated PLGF Levels</div></td><td><div>Elevated PLGF levels may be observed in certain pregnancy-related complications, such as preeclampsia. Preeclampsia is a serious condition characterized by high blood pressure and organ damage, usually occurring after 20 weeks of pregnancy. Elevated PLGF levels in conjunction with other clinical signs may indicate a higher risk for developing preeclampsia. Further evaluation and monitoring by a healthcare professional are necessary.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Low PLGF Levels</div></td><td><div>Low PLGF levels may be associated with poor placental function and compromised fetal growth. Low PLGF levels can be a concern during pregnancy and may indicate a risk of intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) or other pregnancy complications. Additional testing and close monitoring of the pregnancy are necessary in such cases.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
